(((window.HALCYON_DATA=window.HALCYON_DATA||{}).afoqt=window.HALCYON_DATA.afoqt||{}).full=window.HALCYON_DATA.afoqt.full||[]).push({
  num: 2, part: `Part One: The Posting`, title: `Chain of Command`, weeks: `January 11 to February 7, 2027`,
  tasks: [`SJ`,`WK`,`VA`,`AR`,`AI`,`TR`],
  cast: [],
  opening: [
    `On Monday morning Kit came off a night shift at the water plant at seven, drove the river road to the base with the heater on high, and walked into the shop still smelling faintly of chlorine.`,
    `The routing slip had come off the wing's email Sunday night. One page, stapled to the front of her application, with five signature blocks down the right side. The top one said IMMEDIATE SUPERVISOR. She'd filled in everything else at the break table at the plant, at two in the morning between feed checks, and then sat looking at that empty block for a long time, as if it might sign itself.`,
    `Boyd worked on base full time, so on weekdays the shop was his. His office was the one by the tool crib, with a door he never closed and a photo on the wall from 1996: two two eight on a gray morning, and Boyd in front of the nose gear at twenty, skinny as a safety wire.`,
    `She put the package on his desk. <span class="said" data-who="Kit">"That's the pilot package. You're the first block."</span>`,
    `Boyd read the top page. He read the second. Then he set the whole thing in his inbox, on top of a parts request, and put his coffee cup on the corner of it.`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Boyd" data-tone="Gruff, short, noncommittal.">"I'll look at it."</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Kit">"Okay."</span>`,
    `She waited for him to look up. He didn't. On the drive home she counted the weeks to June 1, when her scores had to be on file, and got a little over twenty, and after that she couldn't stop counting.`
  ],
  lesson: {
    title: `Vern's Nav Table: words, and who you talk to first`,
    sections: [
      { h: `Word Knowledge: take it apart`,
        body: [`Word Knowledge is twenty five words in five minutes. Each item is one word in capitals and five choices, and you pick the one closest in meaning. That's twelve seconds apiece. You'll know most of them on sight or not at all, and the points that move are in between, the words you half know.`,
          `<span class="said" data-who="Vern">"I carried a pocket dictionary at the nav table for twenty years. You don't get to carry one in there. You get to carry the pieces."</span>`,
          `When you half know a word, take it apart. Most hard English words are Latin or Greek pieces glued together, and a few dozen pieces cover a lot of the test. Start at the front. Pre is before and post is after. Sub is under, trans is across, re is again, ex is out, and in or un usually means not. Then the middle. Port is carry, dict is say, cred is believe, ver is true, voc is call, fid is faith. Then the end. Ous means full of, less means without, and ify usually means to make.`,
          `Once you have a rough meaning, check its charge. Is the word praising somebody or knocking them? A choice or two usually carries the wrong charge, and you can drop those before you're sure of anything else.`,
          `Then watch for the sound alike. The test likes to offer a choice that shares the front of the word and nothing else, because at twelve seconds your eye reads the first syllable and guesses the rest. It also likes the exact opposite, for the person who remembers the word but not which side it's on.`],
        terms: [[`Prefix`,`A piece at the front of a word that changes its meaning, like sub for under.`],[`Connotation`,`The positive or negative charge a word carries, apart from its dictionary meaning.`]],
        exam: `If a word won't come in twelve seconds, drop the choices with the wrong charge, pick from what's left, and move. A blank scores the same as a wrong answer.` },
      { h: `Verbal Analogies: name the relationship`,
        body: [`Verbal Analogies is twenty five items in eight minutes, a little over nineteen seconds each. Some items give you one pair and half of a second pair, and you finish it. Others give you one pair and five whole pairs, and you pick the pair that matches. It's one skill either way. Say the first pair as a sentence, then run each choice through that same sentence.`,
          `The relationships repeat. Synonyms and opposites. Degree, where one word is a stronger form of the other. Part and whole. A member and its group, like a goose and a gaggle. A tool and its job, or a tool and the person who uses it. A worker and what they make, or where they work. Cause and effect. A type and its category. A person and the quality they lack.`,
          `Two habits catch most misses. The first is direction. If the stem runs part to whole, the answer has to run part to whole too, and if it runs mild to strong, the answer can't run strong to mild. The second is tightness. When two choices both fit, your sentence is too loose. Make it more exact, and one of them drops out.`],
        terms: [[`Stem`,`The pair you're given at the start of an analogy item.`],[`Degree`,`A relationship where one word is a stronger or weaker form of the other, like warm and hot.`]],
        exam: `Wrong choices are often from the right topic with the wrong relationship. A word that belongs in the same room as the stem is no reason to pick it.` },
      { h: `Clocks on the short subtests`,
        body: [`The verbal subtests run fast, and the clock is part of what they measure. With half the time gone on Word Knowledge, you want to be around item thirteen. On analogies the halfway mark is four minutes, and the item number is about the same.`,
          `If you're behind at the halfway mark, don't try to speed up on everything. Spend less on the items you don't know cold. Make your best guess and keep going. Nothing comes off for a wrong answer, so every item should have an answer before time runs out.`,
          `Where you stand is one division. Time left over items left is what each remaining item gets. Do it once, at the halfway mark, and then get back to the words.`],
        terms: [[`Pace`,`Time per item. Time left divided by items left tells you what each remaining item gets.`]],
        exam: `One look at the clock at the halfway point is enough. Watching it on every item spends the seconds you're trying to save.` },
      { h: `Situational Judgment: who you talk to first`,
        body: [`A lot of Situational Judgment items come down to one question: who do you talk to first? The key's answer is steady. The person closest to the problem, directly and in private, before anybody above them.`,
          `That holds when the person is your own supervisor. Say you asked for a training seat or a schedule change and heard nothing back. The most effective move is usually to ask again, plainly, in private, and to ask what's needed. Taking it to their supervisor before you've done that is going over a head, and the key often ranks it least effective. Asking someone else to carry the message for you isn't much better.`,
          `The chain of command is still the right tool, used in order. If a direct try fails, you go to the next level, and you tell the person you're going. You don't skip levels to get a faster answer. The key also likes people who keep others in the loop. If something touches a coworker's work, they should get it from you, not secondhand.`,
          `One exception, the same one I gave you the first night. If something false or unsafe is still in motion, like a bad record headed for a jet, you stop it and report it now. The rule isn't never escalate. It's escalate in the right order.`],
        terms: [[`Chain of command`,`The line of supervisors above you, used one level at a time.`],[`Going over a head`,`Taking a problem to someone's boss before you've raised it with them.`]],
        exam: `If an option has you talking to the person directly and in private, look at it first for most effective. If an option has you going around them, look at it first for least.` },
      { h: `Picking most and least`,
        body: [`Each scenario asks for two picks from the same five actions. Several may be decent. You're ranking them, not hunting for the only good one.`,
          `For most effective, ask who owns the problem and whether the action deals with it at the right level, soon and in private. For least effective, ask which action does the most damage. A lie or going over a head usually does more harm than an action that's only slow. A public correction is often in the middle. Doing nothing can be the least effective pick, but don't assume it is.`],
        terms: [[`Most and least`,`The two picks on every Situational Judgment item. They come from the same list, and one action can't be both.`]],
        exam: `Read all five before you pick either one. The least effective action is easier to see once you've ranked the others.` }
    ]
  },
  scenes: [
    { id:`f2s1`, storyQuiet:true, domain:`Verbal`, task:`WK`, title:`Around and look`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>{const a=((all||{})[1]||{}).picks||{};return [`Tuesday, January 12, at seven o'clock, Kit parked on Cedar Street in front of a brick ranch with a hedge out front that had been dead so long it had gone silver. ${a[11]===undefined||a[11]===1?`Nineteen hundred, he'd said at the diner. She was four minutes early and sat in the truck for three of them.`:`She'd had to find him. There was one Pruett on Cedar Street in the county listings, and Monday night she called it and asked properly, the thing she hadn't managed at the counter. He'd let the line go quiet a long time before he gave her a day and a time.`}`,`Vern opened the door before she knocked. The kitchen had a yellow table, a percolator, and a coffee can full of pencils somebody had sharpened with a knife. Through the doorway she could see the den: a long slanted table under a lamp, the kind navigators plotted on, and a banker's box on the end of it with 2001 written on the side in marker. She'd been seven that summer. She looked at the box a beat too long, and then she didn't ask, the same as at the diner. Somewhere down the hall a television was on low.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Vern">"Sit."</span> He tore a sheet off a legal pad, printed one word on it in his square capitals, and turned it around. CIRCUMSPECT.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Vern">"You get twelve seconds a word on this one. Most words you'll know or you won't. This one, don't guess. Take it apart. What's it mean?"</span>`]},
      opts:[
        {t:`Tell him it means roundabout, going the long way to a point.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Vern">"That's circuitous. Same front end, different animal."</span> He printed CIRCUITOUS under it so she'd see the two side by side.`,
          why:`Same prefix, wrong word. Roundabout's circuitous. Two words with the same front end get parked together on purpose. At twelve seconds you read the front and guess the back. Don't.`},
        {t:`Tell him it means careful, looking at every side before acting.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:3,health:2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Kit">"Careful. Circum is around and spect is look. You look all the way around before you do anything."</span> Vern tapped the pad with his pencil. <span class="said" data-who="Vern">"Most people get the look and stop there. The word's about what you do after."</span> He took the pad back. More capitals.`,
          why:`Circum, around. Spect, look. Glue them and you've got somebody who looks all the way around first. Careful. Wary. Twelve seconds is lots, if you've got the pieces on you. And the trap sitting in the other choices? Circuitous. Same front end, nothing else.`},
        {t:`Tell him it means watching whatever is happening around you.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:1,health:0},
          after:`Vern tipped his hand back and forth. <span class="said" data-who="Vern">"That's the roots talking. Around, look. Fine. What do you call a fella who does that?"</span> <span class="said" data-who="Kit">"Careful."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Vern">"Write it down."</span>`,
          why:`You took it apart fine and quit at the parts. The roots say watching what's around. The word says careful. Ask who the roots are describing and you're there.`},
        {t:`Tell him it means suspicious, always checking up on people.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Vern">"You heard spect and went to suspect. Close. Suspect is looking up from under. This one's looking around."</span> He let her sit with the difference.`,
          why:`Spect lives in suspect too, but suspect has sub in front of it. Both pieces have to match, not one. Circumspect is about caution, and it doesn't distrust anybody.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f2s2`, storyQuiet:true, domain:`Verbal`, task:`VA`, title:`Content`, q:`Which word should Kit write in Vern's blank, so both pairs share a relationship?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Vern set the pad aside and dug an index card out of his shirt pocket, already written, the corner rounded off from riding there. CONTENT IS TO ELATED AS ANNOYED IS TO BLANK.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Vern">"Not content like the stuff in a box. Content like a navigator with a fix that'll do. Elated is three stars crossing in one tidy point on the chart, which happened to me maybe four times in twenty years."</span> He pushed the card at her. <span class="said" data-who="Vern">"Make your sentence."</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Write IRRITATED in the blank.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Vern">"Irritated is annoyed with its shirt tucked in. Is elated just content in a nicer shirt?"</span> She crossed it out.`,
          why:`Irritated is annoyed by another name, the same size. Content and elated aren't two names for one thing. They're one feeling at two sizes, small and large. On a degree item, a synonym sitting in the choices is usually bait.`},
        {t:`Write FURIOUS in the blank.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:4,health:2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Kit">"Elated is way more content. Furious is way more annoyed."</span> She could hear the sentence take the weight of both pairs. Vern put the card back in his shirt pocket and buttoned the flap. <span class="said" data-who="Vern">"Degree. Same feeling, more of it."</span>`,
          why:`Content to elated runs small to big. So annoyed wants its big one, and furious is annoyed with the knob cranked. Check which way the first pair ran. Run yours the same way.`},
        {t:`Write CALM in the blank.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Vern">"Calm's across the street from annoyed."</span> He tapped CONTENT, then ELATED, one after the other, both on the same side. She saw it before he had to say anything else.`,
          why:`Opposites are a real relationship, just not this one. Content and elated sit on the same side of the street. Calm crosses over. It doesn't turn anything up.`},
        {t:`Write BORED in the blank.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Vern">"Bored,"</span> Vern said, as if tasting it. <span class="said" data-who="Vern">"Bored isn't annoyed turned up. Bored is annoyed that fell asleep."</span> She tried the sentence anyway. It sagged in the middle.`,
          why:`Bored's from the same neighborhood. That's it. Whatever sentence fits content and elated won't fit bored.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f2s3`, storyQuiet:true, domain:`Quantitative`, task:`AR`, title:`The egg`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`At twenty to eight Vern got the egg timer out of the drawer by the stove. It was white plastic, with a crack down one side that somebody had glued. He cranked it to eight. Then he dealt twenty five index cards onto the table the way you'd deal gin rummy. Analogies, one to a card, in his capitals.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Vern">"Eight minutes. Same as the real one. Go."</span>`,`She went. Card one was a chisel. She fought it. The second she turned over twice. By the time she thought to check the egg it had ticked down to three, with ten cards face down by her elbow and fifteen still waiting.`,`Vern hadn't moved. <span class="said" data-who="Vern">"Fifteen to go, three minutes on the egg. What does each one get now?"</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Tell him nineteen seconds each, the same pace she started with.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:-1,health:0},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Vern">"That was your pace when you sat down. You're five minutes into my egg."</span> The egg ticked. She did it over.`,
          why:`Nineteen and change is the starting average, four hundred eighty seconds over twenty five cards. You spent well past it on the first ten. Once time's gone, the share left for each remaining item comes down.`},
        {t:`Tell him twenty seconds each for the fifteen that are left.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`Vern wanted to know where twenty came from, and she showed him: three hundred over fifteen. <span class="said" data-who="Vern">"That three hundred's gone, Sergeant. You spent it on a chisel."</span>`,
          why:`Three hundred seconds is the five minutes you already burned. Divide what's still on the egg by the cards still on the table.`},
        {t:`Tell him twelve seconds apiece for the fifteen that are left.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:3,health:3},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Kit">"Twelve. A hundred and eighty seconds, fifteen cards."</span> Vern asked what the first ten had cost her, and she told him thirty apiece. He grunted. The egg kept going. She ran the last fifteen at twelve seconds a card, missed three, and would have missed those three with a week to think.`,
          why:`Time left over items left. A hundred and eighty seconds over fifteen cards is twelve each. The whole subtest is eight minutes for twenty five, a little over nineteen seconds a card, and you spent thirty on each of the first ten. Arithmetic Reasoning likes this shape: an average at the start, a change in the middle, and a question about what's left.`},
        {t:`Tell him eighteen seconds each for the fifteen that are left.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Vern">"That's a hundred and eighty over ten. How many cards are left?"</span> She looked. Fifteen.`,
          why:`A hundred and eighty seconds is right. Ten is the cards you've finished. Divide time left by cards left, every time.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f2s4`, storyQuiet:true, domain:`Verbal`, task:`WK`, title:`Scintilla`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`The egg went off. Vern pushed his chair back, went into the den, and came back with one sheet out of the banker's box. Not the box. He laid it on the table facing her.`,`<prop type="doc" title="Navigator's log, January 2001" say="My log, sixteen January, oh one. Tolliver to the track and home, six point one hours. Celestial check at oh four ten Zulu. Stars scintillating bad below twenty degrees, so I shot the high ones. Lieutenant Merritt, copilot, first track with us. She asked what scintillate means. Told her twinkle. Looked it up at the table in my pocket dictionary. From scintilla, a spark." by="Vern">16 JAN 01. TOLLIVER, TRACK, TOLLIVER. 6.1 HRS.<br>CELESTIAL CHECK 0410Z. STARS SCINTILLATING BAD BELOW 20 DEG. SHOT THE HIGH ONES.<br>LT MERRITT, COPILOT, FIRST TRACK WITH US. ASKED WHAT SCINTILLATE MEANS. TOLD HER TWINKLE.<br>LOOKED IT UP AT THE TABLE, POCKET DICTIONARY. FROM SCINTILLA, A SPARK.</prop>`,`Kit read the name twice. She'd only ever seen it typed, at the bottom of wing emails about parking and the holiday party. <span class="said" data-who="Kit">"The colonel?"</span>`,`<span class="said" data-who="Vern">"She was a lieutenant once. Everybody was. Far as this table goes, she's still the Lieutenant."</span> He tapped the last line. The eraser on his pencil was worn down to the metal. <span class="said" data-who="Vern">"One a week. You don't get the box. Now. Scintilla's a spark. A man tells you there isn't a scintilla of doubt. How much doubt is there?"</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Tell him very little, maybe a small trace of it.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Vern">"A trace is what a scintilla is. Not one of them is how much?"</span> He waited until she said none.`,
          why:`The trace is the scintilla. Not a scintilla is less than that. Watch the not. At twelve seconds a word you'll step right over it.`},
        {t:`Tell him enough to start an argument going.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Vern">"That's the spark catching. There isn't even the spark."</span> He drew a match on the pad, unlit.`,
          why:`You struck the match and burned the barn down. It's the tiniest bit going, and the not takes that too.`},
        {t:`Tell him a sharp doubt that flares up fast.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Vern">"You're picturing it. Don't picture it. Count it. Not a single spark is how many?"</span> <span class="said" data-who="Kit">"None."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Vern">"There you go."</span>`,
          why:`You saw the spark. Fine. How big was it? Scintilla's an amount, a pinch, and nobody asks what a pinch looks like.`},
        {t:`Tell him none at all, not even a spark.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:3,health:2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Kit">"None. Not even a spark's worth."</span> Vern creased the log down the middle with his thumbnail and handed it across. <span class="said" data-who="Vern">"Next Tuesday, three words with the same front end as circumspect. Out of your head. Not out of a book."</span> She put the log in her coat with the diner napkin.`,
          why:`A scintilla's one spark, about as small as amounts get. Stick not in front and it's zero. They like to hide a root you know inside a word you don't, so dig it out, then mind the little words next to it. Not an iota. Not a shred. Same deal.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f2s5`, domain:`Judgment`, task:`SJ`, title:`Inbox`, q:`The slip has sat ten days with Boyd's block empty. What should Kit do?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>{const a=((all||{})[1]||{}).picks||{};return [`Ten days later the slip was still in Boyd's inbox. She'd had two Tuesdays at the yellow table by then, and two logs in her coat.`,`Thursday, January 21, she had a day off from the plant and drove out to the base for no reason she'd have admitted to anybody. Midweek the shop ran on four people and a radio. She walked past his office to the coffee pot, and there it was, under the same cup, with a second coffee ring on it now beside the first. The top block was empty. ${a[6]===undefined||a[6]===3?`She'd told him he'd hear it from her first, and he had. He'd heard it and put a cup on it.`:a[6]===0?`On the January drill she'd told him she was only reading the posting. The slip had told him otherwise, and he hadn't said a word about that either.`:`He hadn't said a word about it since she'd put it on his desk.`}`,`Down the hall the first sergeant's door stood open, with a bowl of peppermints on the desk, there for exactly the kind of airman who had a problem with her supervisor. Across the street, in the headquarters building with the flags out front, the colonel's exec kept her calendar on a very clean desk. Kit thought about both of them before she'd finished pouring.`,`Boyd was on the phone in his office with the door open, arguing with somebody about a parts delivery.`]},
      opts:[
        {t:`Ask him about it at the coffee pot, with the shop around them.`, s:1, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Kit">"Sergeant Boyd, you get a chance at my slip?"</span> Three people at the coffee pot find something to read. Boyd looks at her over the phone. <span class="said" data-who="Boyd">"When I get to it,"</span> he says, and goes back to his call.`,
          why:`Right man, wrong room. Fair question, but at the coffee pot it went public, and he answered for whoever was listening. Ask it behind a shut door and you'd get something else.`},
        {t:`Take a copy of the package down the hall to the first sergeant.`, s:0, flag:`wentoverboyd`, d:{trust:-4,conf:-1,health:-1},
          after:`The first sergeant hears her out and reaches for the phone with her still sitting there. Boyd's extension. It rings down the hall. He picks up. On her way back past his office, his door's shut.`,
          why:`A first sergeant is there for a lot of things. Your flight chief's inbox isn't one of them until you've asked him yourself. Going over a head before you've tried the person is a move the test often keys least effective, and in a shop it's the kind of thing people remember for years.`},
        {t:`Wait out his call, then shut the door and ask him straight.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:2,health:2},
          after:`She waited out the call, all of it, including the part where he spelled his own name twice for the parts guy. Then she stepped in and shut the door. Boyd looked at the door before he looked at her. <span class="said" data-who="Kit" data-tone="Quiet, clear, direct.">"Is it the slip, or is it me?"</span> Boyd studied the coffee ring as if it were a leak he had to trace. <span class="said" data-who="Boyd" data-tone="Low, gruff, reluctant.">"It isn't you."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Kit">"Then what is it?"</span> <span class="said" data-who="Boyd" data-tone="Low, gruff, reluctant.">"It's a lot of names after mine, Sergeant."</span> He slid the cup off the corner and onto the parts request. He didn't pick the slip up. <span class="said" data-who="Boyd" data-tone="Low, gruff, reluctant.">"Give me till drill."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Kit">"Okay."</span> She did mean it. He could see that, and it seemed to make it worse for him.`,
          why:`Leadership and Communication. His block's the top one, so his is the first conversation, and you had it with the door shut instead of across a coffee pot. You asked what was wrong instead of telling him what he owed you. Did he sign? No. A direct try doesn't promise a yes. It earns you the next step if you need one, and you haven't needed one yet.`},
        {t:`Email the colonel's exec and ask for fifteen minutes with her.`, s:0, flag:`wentoverboyd`, d:{trust:-4,conf:-1,health:-1},
          after:`The reply comes back in forty minutes, polite and short. Routing begins with the applicant's immediate supervisor. Boyd's name is on the copy line. That afternoon he walks past her at the coffee pot and doesn't stop.`,
          why:`You skipped three levels to get onto a calendar. An exec's job is to send you back down the chain, and that's what happened, with your flight chief copied. The chain is for after a direct try fails, and you hadn't made one.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f2s6`, storyQuiet:true, domain:`Science and Aviation`, task:`AI`, title:`Pitot and static`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>{const a=((all||{})[1]||{}).picks||{};return [`The avionics back shop smelled like solder and hot dust. Corbin Haas was at the far bench with an airspeed indicator clamped in a fixture and two hoses running from it to a test set, watching the needle climb.`,`He was thirty four, a technical sergeant, and two years ago the wing had sent him to pilot training. That part everybody knew.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Corbin">"Darrow,"</span> he said to the needle. ${a[8]===undefined||a[8]===0?`<span class="said" data-who="Corbin">"Kemp showed up Monday with a donut and an apology. Took him a while to say whose idea it was. I figure I owe you half the donut."</span>`:a[8]===3?`<span class="said" data-who="Corbin">"Somebody tacked a note about me on your corkboard. Now the guys in fuels know my name."</span> He didn't ask if it was her. She didn't say.`:a[8]===2?`<span class="said" data-who="Corbin">"Heard you told the break room my business was mine. Appreciate it. Didn't help much."</span>`:`<span class="said" data-who="Corbin">"Heard what Kemp's been saying. Heard you were in the room."</span>`} <span class="said" data-who="Corbin">"Word on the ramp is you put in for it."</span>`,`<span class="said" data-who="Kit">"It's in Boyd's inbox. Under a coffee cup."</span>`,`<span class="said" data-who="Corbin">"Mine sat under a stapler for a month."</span> He nodded at the shelf over the bench, where three instruments sat in a row with yellow repair tags hanging off them: an airspeed indicator, an altimeter, a vertical speed indicator. <span class="said" data-who="Corbin">"Since they're going to ask you anyway. Pitot side, static side. Which of those three needs both?"</span>`]},
      opts:[
        {t:`The vertical speed indicator. It reads how fast she climbs.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Corbin">"Vertical speed's a static only gauge. It sits there watching the pressure drift."</span> He held up one finger, shiny with solder flux. <span class="said" data-who="Corbin">"One of those three needs the pitot."</span>`,
          why:`The vertical speed indicator watches one pressure change over time, the static one. Go up and the static drops. Come down and it rises. The pitot tube never gets a vote.`},
        {t:`The airspeed indicator. It compares ram air to still air.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:3,health:2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Kit">"Airspeed. Ram air up the pitot tube, quiet air off the static port, and the needle's the difference."</span> Corbin let go of the knob on the test set. <span class="said" data-who="Corbin">"Ice up the pitot and you've got one liar on the panel."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Kit">"Which one?"</span> <span class="said" data-who="Corbin">"You just told me which one."</span>`,
          why:`I sat at the nav table behind a copilot once, in freezing rain out of Tolliver, who glared at his airspeed needle like it owed him money. Picture the pitot tube as a soda straw jammed into the wind. The static port's a pinhole on the skin, minding its own business. Airspeed gets a line from each and reads the difference. Altimeter and vertical speed drink static only, so that night they were still honest.`},
        {t:`All three of them. They're the pitot static instruments.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Corbin">"All three take static. Only one takes pitot."</span> He waited while she got there.`,
          why:`Half right is how good mechanics get bit. Every one of the three eats static. Pitot belongs to airspeed alone, so a question that says both is pointing at one gauge.`},
        {t:`The altimeter. It has to know how high and how fast.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Corbin">"Altimeter wouldn't know fast if it hit it. Static only."</span> He tapped the airspeed indicator in the fixture. <span class="said" data-who="Corbin">"This one cares."</span>`,
          why:`An altimeter is a barometer with a dial bolted on. It reads static pressure and calls it feet. Nobody ever asked it how fast.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f2s7`, domain:`Judgment`, task:`SJ`, title:`The bars`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`He shut the test set off. For a while he just coiled the hose.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Corbin">"Everybody wants to know what happened. Nobody asks. They ask Kemp."</span> He hung the hose over a nail. <span class="said" data-who="Corbin" data-tone="Quiet, rueful, honest.">"I was good on paper. Good in the books. Then they strap you in with an instructor behind you who writes down every single thing your hands do, and pretty soon you're flying his clipboard instead of the airplane."</span>`,`He worked a rag over his knuckles, one at a time.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Corbin" data-tone="Quiet, level, not asking for anything.">"I gave the bars back. Shop took me home."</span>`,`Kemp had told that story a dozen ways in the break room. None of them had the bars in it.`,`He looked at her then, straight on, the way he'd check a torque stripe. <span class="said" data-who="Corbin" data-tone="Direct, serious, kind.">"So what happens to you if you wash out? Somebody ought to ask you that before the board does."</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Tell him she isn't planning on washing out at all.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:-1,health:0},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Corbin">"Nobody plans it."</span> He turned back to the bench.`,
          why:`Confident, and it answers a question he didn't ask. He asked what happens if it goes wrong, because he's the one man in the wing who knows. Saying it won't go wrong is how you skip the question without seeming to.`},
        {t:`Ask him what the colonel got wrong when she picked him.`, s:0, d:{trust:-3,conf:-1,health:0},
          after:`Corbin's face closed up like an access panel. <span class="said" data-who="Corbin">"Ask her."</span> He went back to the bench, and whatever he'd been about to tell her stayed in the shop.`,
          why:`You asked a man to grade his commander, in her own wing, with her office across the street. That's gossip in uniform. The test keys it low.`},
        {t:`Tell him she'd come home like he did, and ask what he'd change.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:3,health:2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Kit" data-tone="Quiet, clear, a little surprised.">"I come home and crew a jet. Same as you."</span> She hadn't known that was the answer until it was out. <span class="said" data-who="Kit">"What would you do different?"</span> Corbin took his time with it. <span class="said" data-who="Corbin" data-tone="Slow, honest, rueful.">"I'd have told somebody the truth sooner. I told everybody it was going fine, right up to the day it wasn't."</span> He picked up the next indicator. <span class="said" data-who="Corbin">"Doesn't help you with the test."</span> It didn't. It helped with something else, and she drove home with it.`,
          why:`He asked what everybody else was too polite to. You answered it plainly, then asked what he knows. Mentoring runs uphill sometimes, and nobody else in this wing has been where you're headed. On the test that's how you treat someone with experience you don't have: answer straight, ask, then listen. He gave you more than a test tip, didn't he?`},
        {t:`Tell him she's sorry about what happened to him at pilot training.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-1,health:0},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Corbin">"Yeah,"</span> he said. <span class="said" data-who="Corbin">"Everybody is."</span> He meant it kindly, and it ended the conversation anyway.`,
          why:`Kind, and it puts him back in the middle of the story he'd just stepped out of. He didn't ask for sympathy. He asked a question. Answer it.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f2s8`, domain:`Verbal`, task:`VA`, title:`Insulator`, q:`Which pair has the same relationship as INSULATOR and CURRENT?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`On Friday, February 5, the fridge whiteboard slid off its bracket and down the door with the whole week still on it. Kit went out to the garage for the drill.`,`The garage was cold enough to see her breath. The drill hung on the pegboard and its battery didn't. She looked on the workbench, then behind the mower, where things went to be forgotten. Back against the wall there was a blue tarp over something with corners, about as tall as her hip. Graham's, she figured. A school thing. She found the battery in a jar of screws on the shelf and went back in.`,`Graham already had the whiteboard back up, with a butter knife. The drill sat on the counter, unneeded. He was at the table with a stack of index cards held together by a rubber band, made on his lunch period in the printing he used on lab quizzes. A hundred of them, he said. Words on the front, meanings on the back, and some analogies he'd written himself.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Graham">"When do you book the test?"</span>`,`<span class="said" data-who="Kit">"When the slip's signed."</span>`,`<span class="said" data-who="Graham" data-tone="Mild, easy, matter-of-fact.">"After the slip, then."</span> He said it the way he wrote on the whiteboard, one more block in the week. She heard it go up next to the degree and the inspection, and she let it stay there. Then he turned over the first card. INSULATOR : CURRENT. <span class="said" data-who="Graham">"Pick the pair that matches. I'll read you four."</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Pick PIPE : WATER.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Graham">"A pipe carries water. Does an insulator carry current?"</span> <span class="said" data-who="Kit">"It does the opposite."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Graham">"Then it's the opposite pair."</span>`,
          why:`A pipe carries water. That's what a conductor does, not an insulator. Opposite pairs show up in these on purpose. Check which direction the job runs, not just the topic.`},
        {t:`Pick SPONGE : WATER.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Graham">"Close."</span> He tapped the card. <span class="said" data-who="Graham">"A sponge soaks water up and holds on to it. An insulator doesn't take any current in. It just won't let it through."</span>`,
          why:`A sponge keeps water from spreading by drinking it. An insulator never takes the current in at all. Both stop a flow, which is why it feels close, but only one pair fits the sentence "blocks the flow of." Keep the tighter sentence.`},
        {t:`Pick DAM : RIVER.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:3,health:2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Kit">"An insulator stops current. A dam stops a river."</span> Graham turned the card over to check his own answer, which made her laugh. <span class="said" data-who="Graham">"Sorry. Habit."</span>`,
          why:`An insulator blocks the flow of current. A dam blocks the flow of a river. One sentence, both pairs. The pick a pair items are the same skill run backward: build the sentence from the stem, then push each pair through it. The pair that fits the sentence tightest wins.`},
        {t:`Pick SWITCH : LAMP.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Graham">"A switch can stop current,"</span> he allowed. <span class="said" data-who="Graham">"It can start it too. An insulator never does."</span>`,
          why:`A switch controls a circuit both ways. An insulator only ever blocks. A pair that fits half the time doesn't fit.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f2s9`, domain:`Judgment`, task:`SJ`, title:`Who gets her`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Saturday was drill. At 5:40 in the morning Kit and Fisk sat in the crew truck with the heater roaring, waiting on the forms. Fisk still had the warehouse scanner on its lanyard, bumping his chest every time he shifted. Kemp's two airmen were in the back seat with their hoods up, faking sleep about as well as anybody does at that hour.`,`Fisk had been quiet since the gate. Then he said it to the windshield. <span class="said" data-who="Fisk">"Kemp says you're going to be a pilot."</span>`,`She let the wipers go across twice before she said anything, and he turned in his seat. <span class="said" data-who="Fisk">"So who gets two two eight?"</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Answer him right there, with Kemp's airmen listening.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:1,health:0},
          after:`She tells him. All of it's true, and all of it's in front of two airmen who aren't asleep. By lunch it's Darrow's leaving and Fisk's getting the jet, and neither half is quite right.`,
          why:`The truth was right. The audience wasn't. A personal answer told in front of other people's airmen is a broadcast. Ten feet away, the same words would've stayed his.`},
        {t:`Promise him she isn't going anywhere, so he can relax.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-1,health:-1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Kit">"I'm not going anywhere."</span> Fisk relaxes. She watches him do it and knows she'll have to take it back.`,
          why:`It's a lie, a kind one, and you'll pay for it the day the board meets. The test keys dishonesty low even when it's meant to comfort somebody.`},
        {t:`Tell him that's above his pay grade, and to get to work.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:0,health:-1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Fisk">"Yes, Sergeant."</span> He goes for the forms. He doesn't ask her another question all morning, which isn't like him.`,
          why:`He asked about his own job. Shutting him down teaches him not to ask you things, and that's the opposite of what you'll need from him later.`},
        {t:`Walk him away from the truck and tell him the truth.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:2,health:2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Kit">"Come help me with the chocks."</span> Out by the nose wheel the wind came straight off the river and took every word sideways. <span class="said" data-who="Kit">"I put in for it. Odds are lousy and it's months out. If it happens, I want her going to you."</span> Fisk looked at the jet as if he'd never seen it before. <span class="said" data-who="Fisk" data-tone="Surprised, small, disbelieving.">"Me?"</span> <span class="said" data-who="Kit" data-tone="Dry, teasing, warm.">"Who else knows where my flashlight is?"</span> That got a laugh out of him, a short one that fogged in front of his face. <span class="said" data-who="Kit">"So we start on the things I don't write down."</span>`,
          why:`Mentoring and Communication. He asked about his own future, so he gets a straight answer, away from people who'd turn it into a story. You told him what you knew and what you didn't, and then you handed him a job. He can put weight on that, even if this whole thing falls through. Was any of it a promise you can't keep? No.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f2s10`, domain:`Speed and Spatial`, task:`TR`, title:`Wind chill`, q:`It's 10 degrees with a 15 mile an hour wind. What should Kit read off the card for Fisk?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`By ten the wind had swung around out of the northwest. The thermometer screwed to the hangar door said 10, and nobody believed it was that warm. Boyd had put tire servicing on two two eight on the morning board, so it was going to be Kit and Fisk kneeling on frozen concrete by the main gear for an hour, maybe longer.`,`Every truck had the shop's cold weather card taped to the dash, curling at one corner from the defroster. Wind chill chart on top: wind speed in miles an hour along the top edge, air temperature down the left, a wind chill in each box. Under it, the shop's warm up rule, printed on label maker tape.`,`<prop type="doc" title="Cold weather card, taped inside the crew truck" say="The cold weather card off the dash. Wind speed runs along the top, air temperature down the left side, and each box holds the wind chill. Underneath, the shop's warm up rule. Above minus five, every hour. Minus five to minus fourteen, every thirty minutes. Minus fifteen or colder, every fifteen.">WIND CHILL, DEGREES F. Columns: wind, mph. Rows: air temperature, degrees F.<br>TEMP · 5 · 10 · 15 · 20 · 25<br>20 · 13 · 9 · 6 · 4 · 3<br>15 · 7 · 3 · 0 · -2 · -4<br>10 · 1 · -4 · -7 · -9 · -11<br>5 · -5 · -10 · -13 · -15 · -17<br>0 · -11 · -16 · -19 · -22 · -24<br>SHOP RULE: above -5, warm up every hour. -5 to -14, every 30 minutes. -15 or colder, every 15 minutes.</prop>`,`The flag by base ops was standing straight out, and the weather on her phone said wind fifteen.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Read minus 4, and call warm ups every hour.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-1,health:-3},
          after:`She reads minus 4 and calls an hour. That's the ten mile an hour column. They're on their knees at the gear for fifty minutes before Fisk can't feel the valve cap anymore.`,
          why:`You slid a column left, into ten miles an hour. Wind runs across the top, temperature down the side. Find both headings first, then move.`},
        {t:`Read minus 9, and call warm ups every 30 minutes.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`She reads minus 9 and calls thirty minutes. She'd slid one column right, into twenty miles an hour, and the band happened to catch her. Right break, wrong cell.`,
          why:`One column over. The shop's bands caught you this time, so the break came out right. On the test nothing catches you. Some of the five choices are the neighbors, and one over is wrong.`},
        {t:`Read minus 7, and call warm ups every 30 minutes.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:3,health:4},
          after:`Finger on the 10 row, finger on the 15 column, slide. Minus 7. She checked next door both ways, minus 4 and minus 9, so she'd know what a slip would look like. <span class="said" data-who="Kit">"Thirty minutes on, then the truck. I'll keep the clock."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Fisk">"I'm fine, Sergeant."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Kit">"You've been up since yesterday. Thirty minutes."</span>`,
          why:`Ten degrees is a row. Fifteen miles an hour is a column. The answer lives where they cross, and the cells around it are the traps: minus 4 to the left, minus 9 to the right, 0 above, minus 13 below. Table Reading builds many of its wrong answers out of the neighbors. Anchor both headings before you read a number.`},
        {t:`Read 0, and call warm ups every hour.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-1,health:-3},
          after:`She reads 0, which is the row above, at 15 degrees. They work an hour straight in a wind chill of minus 7, and Fisk can't feel his fingertips when they finally climb into the truck.`,
          why:`That's the 15 degree row, one up. A one row slip is an easy miss on a grid like this, and here it nearly cost two people their fingertips.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f2s11`, storyQuiet:true, domain:`Judgment`, task:`SJ`, title:`Line walk`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>{const p=s.picks||{};const wob=(s.flags||{}).wentoverboyd||(G||{}).wentoverboyd;return [`At two that afternoon the wing commander came down the line on foot, one jet at a time, with a cup of coffee and nobody trailing her.`,`Colonel Joanna Merritt was forty nine and still flew the KC-135 herself a few times a month. She stopped under the pilot's window of two two eight and read the stencil, CREW CHIEF SSGT K. DARROW, as if she were reading a roster.`,`It took Kit a second to match her to a line of block capitals on Vern's log. LT MERRITT, COPILOT, FIRST TRACK WITH US.`,`${wob?(p[4]===1?`<span class="said" data-who="Merritt">"Sergeant Darrow. Your first sergeant came to see me about a package that's stuck in your flight."</span>`:p[4]===3?`<span class="said" data-who="Merritt">"Sergeant Darrow. My exec tells me you wanted time on my calendar."</span>`:`<span class="said" data-who="Merritt">"Sergeant Darrow. I hear you've been asking around my headquarters about a package."</span>`):`<span class="said" data-who="Merritt">"Sergeant Darrow. I hear you're interested in my pilot slot."</span>`} <span class="said" data-who="Merritt">"Nothing's come across my desk. Where is it?"</span>`]},
      opts:[
        {t:`Offer to bring her a copy Monday, so she can read it early.`, s:1, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Merritt">"It'll come through your chain, Sergeant,"</span> Merritt said, not unkindly. <span class="said" data-who="Merritt">"I'll read it when it does."</span>`,
          why:`Right in spirit. You want her to see it. But a copy that skips your chain asks her to go around your flight chief for you, and she told you so, kindly.`},
        {t:`Tell her it's only shop talk, ma'am, and nothing's ready.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-2,health:0},
          after:`Merritt looked up at the stencil, then back at her. <span class="said" data-who="Merritt">"Shop talk doesn't usually stand under my jets reading its own name, Sergeant."</span>`,
          why:`She asked a straight question and you ducked it, with your name stenciled two feet over your head. Honest and short beats modest and false.`},
        {t:`Tell her Boyd has had it since January 11 and hasn't signed.`, s:0, d:{trust:-3,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`Merritt's face didn't change at all, which was worse. <span class="said" data-who="Merritt">"Then I imagine I'll hear about that from him."</span>`,
          why:`It's true, and it's a complaint about your flight chief, delivered to his commander while he isn't there. The test calls that briefing the boss behind someone's back, and so does every flight chief.`},
        {t:`Tell her it's with her flight chief and coming up through him.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:3,health:2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Kit">"It's with my flight chief, ma'am. It'll come to you through him."</span> Merritt didn't say anything for a moment. <span class="said" data-who="Merritt">"Good. That's where it belongs."</span>`,
          why:`Communication, and a loyalty that cost you nothing. She asked where it was, and you told her the truth without handing her your flight chief. Briefing the boss behind someone's back is a move the test marks down. You can be honest about where a thing sits without turning it into a complaint.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f2s12`, domain:`Judgment`, task:`SJ`, title:`The door`, q:`Boyd knows the colonel stopped at the jet, but not what was said. What should Kit do?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>{const p=s.picks||{};const wob=(s.flags||{}).wentoverboyd||(G||{}).wentoverboyd;return [`Merritt didn't move on. She turned the cup a quarter turn in her hand.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Merritt" data-tone="Clear, serious, searching.">"Tell me why. Not why you deserve it."</span>`,`Kit had an answer. She'd put it together at the corkboard in January and been sanding on it ever since. <span class="said" data-who="Kit" data-tone="Polished, careful, rehearsed.">"The wing's going to need pilots who know the jet from the outside, ma'am. When the new jets come, somebody who crewed the old ones ought to be up front."</span>`,`It came out whole. The other answer she'd said once, in her own kitchen, and it had come out about the size of a seven year old. That one wasn't for the flight line.`,`Merritt let her finish. Then she nodded once. <span class="said" data-who="Merritt" data-tone="Cool, even, not unkind.">"That's a good reason for the wing to want you,"</span> she said. <span class="said" data-who="Merritt" data-tone="Cool, even, not unkind.">"It isn't what I asked."</span> She went on to the next jet. Kit stayed under her own name a while, the wind going straight through her coat.`,`Sunday morning Boyd's door was open again. By then the whole ramp knew the colonel had stood at two two eight for six minutes. Nobody but Kit knew what she'd said there. ${wob?`He already knew about January. `:``}${p[10]===2?`She'd told the colonel he'd had it since the eleventh and hadn't signed. He didn't know that yet.`:p[10]===1?`She'd told the colonel it was only shop talk, standing under her own name. He didn't know that yet.`:p[10]===0?`She'd offered the colonel a copy around him, and been told no. He didn't know that yet.`:`She'd told the colonel it was with him and coming up through him. He didn't know that yet.`}`]},
      opts:[
        {t:`Text him a short summary of what the colonel asked her.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`She texts him from the parking lot, four lines. It shows as read. The dots come up, go away, come up again, and then there's nothing.`,
          why:`The words were right and the medium wasn't. He was forty feet away. A text says you'd rather not see his face while he reads it.`},
        {t:`Go to his office and tell him everything the colonel asked.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:3,health:2},
          after:`She was still reaching for the door when Boyd got up and shut it himself. <span class="said" data-who="Kit">"The colonel stopped at the jet yesterday and asked where my package was."</span> She told him what she'd said back, all of it. Boyd waited. <span class="said" data-who="Kit" data-tone="Quiet, plain, rueful.">"Then she asked me why, and I gave her the wing."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Boyd">"What'd she say?"</span> <span class="said" data-who="Kit" data-tone="Quiet, plain, rueful.">"That it wasn't what she asked."</span> Boyd looked at his inbox. Something moved in his face and didn't make it out.`,
          why:`Communication. When you've talked with someone's boss, you tell that someone yourself, first and in private. He shouldn't learn what his own airman told the colonel from the ramp. You also gave him the part that didn't go well, and that's what makes the rest believable.`},
        {t:`Tell him the colonel's interested, so he ought to sign it now.`, s:0, d:{trust:-3,conf:-1,health:0},
          after:`Boyd leaned back in his chair. <span class="said" data-who="Boyd">"You want me to sign it because the colonel looked at your jet?"</span> She didn't, really. It was too late to say so.`,
          why:`You turned the colonel into a lever. Using the boss's interest to push your supervisor is going over his head with better manners, and it doesn't feel polite from his chair.`},
        {t:`Say nothing, and let the colonel tell him if she wants.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-1,health:0},
          after:`She says nothing. Boyd hears it at noon on the ramp, as a story with the colonel and Darrow and the wind in it, and he hears it last.`,
          why:`Leaving it to the commander means he hears it from above or from the ramp, and either way not from you. Closing the loop is your job, not hers.`}
      ]}
  ],
  exercise: { after:`f2s1`, title:`Take it apart`, intro:`Vern wrote six more words down the legal pad, each with one piece he wanted her to name. Pick what that piece means.`,
    items:[
      {prompt:`CIRCUMNAVIGATE: circum`, choices:[`Around`,`Under`,`Across`], answer:0, why:`Circum is around. To circumnavigate is to sail or fly all the way around something.`},
      {prompt:`PORTABLE: port`, choices:[`Door`,`Carry`,`Harbor`], answer:1, why:`Port here is carry, as in transport. Portable means easy to carry. The harbor and the door are other words that happen to share the letters.`},
      {prompt:`DICTATE: dict`, choices:[`Rule`,`Write`,`Say`], answer:2, why:`Dict is say. To dictate is to say something aloud for someone else to write down.`},
      {prompt:`INCREDIBLE: cred`, choices:[`Believe`,`Earn`,`Grow`], answer:0, why:`Cred is believe. In means not, so incredible is hard to believe.`},
      {prompt:`SUBMERGE: sub`, choices:[`Over`,`Under`,`Again`], answer:1, why:`Sub is under. To submerge is to put something under water.`},
      {prompt:`MALFUNCTION: mal`, choices:[`Many`,`Small`,`Bad`], answer:2, why:`Mal is bad. A malfunction is a part working badly or not at all.`}
    ]},
  quiz: [
    {q:`LUCID: closest in meaning`, opts:[`clear`,`calm`,`simple`,`brief`,`lucky`], a:0, why:`Lucid means clear and easy to understand, like a lucid explanation. Simple and brief often go with clear writing, but a long explanation can still be lucid. Lucky only looks like it.`, task:`WK`, domain:`Verbal`},
    {q:`VERBOSE: closest in meaning`, opts:[`vocal`,`wordy`,`fluent`,`eager`,`brief`], a:1, why:`Verbose means using more words than needed. The verb root is word, as in verbal. Vocal and fluent are about speaking up or speaking well, not speaking too much. Brief is the opposite.`, task:`WK`, domain:`Verbal`},
    {q:`BENEVOLENT: closest in meaning`, opts:[`polite`,`cheerful`,`kind`,`humble`,`wicked`], a:2, why:`Bene is good and vol is wish, so benevolent is wishing others well. Kind is the match. Polite and humble are good qualities too, but they're about manners and pride, not goodwill. Wicked has the opposite charge.`, task:`WK`, domain:`Verbal`},
    {q:`INNOCUOUS: closest in meaning`, opts:[`innocent`,`gentle`,`hidden`,`harmless`,`minor`], a:3, why:`Innocuous means harmless, not likely to hurt or offend. In is not and the root means harm. Innocent sounds like it and lives nearby, but innocent is about guilt, not harm. Gentle and minor are close in feel and miss the root.`, task:`WK`, domain:`Verbal`},
    {q:`CHRONIC: closest in meaning`, opts:[`painful`,`severe`,`serious`,`timely`,`lasting`], a:4, why:`Chronic means long lasting or coming back again and again. The chron root is time. Painful and severe are words it often sits next to, not its meaning, and timely shares the root but means on time.`, task:`WK`, domain:`Verbal`},
    {q:`TRANSIENT: closest in meaning`, opts:[`passing`,`sudden`,`changing`,`distant`,`steady`], a:0, why:`Transient means lasting only a short time. Trans is across, so it's something passing through. Sudden is about how a thing starts, not how long it stays, and changing isn't the same as leaving. Steady is the opposite.`, task:`WK`, domain:`Verbal`},
    {q:`CONCEDE: closest in meaning`, opts:[`defend`,`admit`,`conceal`,`approve`,`apologize`], a:1, why:`To concede is to admit something is true, often after resisting. Apologize goes past admitting into saying you're sorry, and approve means you like it, which conceding doesn't. Conceal starts the same way and means to hide, the sound alike trap.`, task:`WK`, domain:`Verbal`},
    {q:`PLACATE: closest in meaning`, opts:[`flatter`,`persuade`,`soothe`,`place`,`provoke`], a:2, why:`To placate is to calm someone who's angry. Soothe is the match. Flatter and persuade are ways of working on someone, but neither one calms them down. Provoke is the opposite, and place only shares the letters.`, task:`WK`, domain:`Verbal`},
    {q:`FAMISHED is to HUNGRY as EXHAUSTED is to`, opts:[`rested`,`awake`,`busy`,`tired`,`sick`], a:3, why:`Degree. Famished is extremely hungry, and exhausted is extremely tired. The pairs run strong to mild in the same direction. Rested is the opposite.`, task:`VA`, domain:`Verbal`},
    {q:`FLOCK is to SHEEP as SWARM is to`, opts:[`honey`,`hive`,`flower`,`wings`,`bees`], a:4, why:`Group to member. A flock is a group of sheep, and a swarm is a group of bees. A hive is where bees live, which is a different relationship.`, task:`VA`, domain:`Verbal`},
    {q:`PEN is to WRITE as KNIFE is to`, opts:[`cut`,`fork`,`steel`,`kitchen`,`sharpen`], a:0, why:`Tool to its job. You write with a pen and cut with a knife. You sharpen a knife, but that's something done to it, not what it does.`, task:`VA`, domain:`Verbal`},
    {q:`SCARCE is to PLENTIFUL as TIMID is to`, opts:[`shy`,`bold`,`quiet`,`weak`,`kind`], a:1, why:`Opposites. Scarce is the opposite of plentiful, and timid is the opposite of bold. Shy is a synonym, the usual trap.`, task:`VA`, domain:`Verbal`},
    {q:`BAKER is to BREAD as AUTHOR is to`, opts:[`pen`,`library`,`novel`,`reader`,`editor`], a:2, why:`Worker to product. A baker makes bread, and an author writes a novel. A pen is the author's tool, and a library is where the product ends up.`, task:`VA`, domain:`Verbal`},
    {q:`Choose the pair that has the same relationship as OVERWORK : FATIGUE.`, opts:[`doctor : patient`,`salt : pepper`,`hammer : nail`,`study : learning`,`rain : cloud`], a:3, why:`Cause and effect. Overwork causes fatigue, and study causes learning. Rain and clouds are related too, but the pair runs backward: the cloud comes first.`, task:`VA`, domain:`Verbal`},
    {q:`SPARROW is to BIRD as SALMON is to`, opts:[`river`,`scales`,`trout`,`swim`,`fish`], a:4, why:`Type to category. A sparrow is a kind of bird, and a salmon is a kind of fish. Trout is another type on the same level, not the category.`, task:`VA`, domain:`Verbal`},
    {q:`Choose the pair that has the same relationship as CAPTAIN : SHIP.`, opts:[`conductor : orchestra`,`passenger : airliner`,`author : manuscript`,`anchor : boat`,`sailor : ocean`], a:0, why:`Leader to what they lead. A captain commands a ship, and a conductor leads an orchestra. A passenger rides an airliner but doesn't run it.`, task:`VA`, domain:`Verbal`},
    {q:`You're a new lieutenant. Three weeks ago you asked your flight commander, a captain, to approve your seat in a two week course. You've heard nothing, and the course fills next month. Which action is MOST effective?`, opts:[`Ask the captain's admin to move it to the top of the pile.`,`Ask the captain for a few minutes in private about what's needed.`,`Mention at the staff meeting that your request is still waiting.`,`Drop the course and hope another seat opens next year.`,`Email the squadron commander and ask them to approve it.`], a:1, why:`Direct and private, at the lowest level. The captain owns the decision, so you ask the captain, and you ask what's needed rather than demanding a signature. Bringing it up at the staff meeting makes it public.`, task:`SJ`, domain:`Judgment`},
    {q:`You're a new lieutenant. Three weeks ago you asked your flight commander, a captain, to approve your seat in a two week course. You've heard nothing, and the course fills next month. Which action is LEAST effective?`, opts:[`Ask the captain's admin to move it to the top of the pile.`,`Ask the captain for a few minutes in private about what's needed.`,`Mention at the staff meeting that your request is still waiting.`,`Drop the course and hope another seat opens next year.`,`Email the squadron commander and ask them to approve it.`], a:4, why:`Going over the captain's head before you've said a word to them is least effective. It skips a level, it tells the commander you can't work with your own supervisor, and the captain will hear about it.`, task:`SJ`, domain:`Judgment`},
    {q:`You're a lieutenant. Another lieutenant at your level keeps booking their airmen on your section's test equipment without asking, and twice this week your crew has waited an hour. Which action is MOST effective?`, opts:[`Lock the equipment and keep the only key yourself.`,`Have your crew tell the other airmen to stay off it.`,`Tell your commander the other lieutenant ignores the schedule.`,`Talk with the other lieutenant one on one and agree on sharing it.`,`Post a sign up sheet on the equipment for everyone to use.`], a:3, why:`A peer problem goes to the peer first, privately. You fix the cause, which is two sections with no agreement, and you keep the relationship. A sign up sheet might help later, but it skips the conversation.`, task:`SJ`, domain:`Judgment`},
    {q:`You're a lieutenant. Another lieutenant at your level keeps booking their airmen on your section's test equipment without asking, and twice this week your crew has waited an hour. Which action is LEAST effective?`, opts:[`Lock the equipment and keep the only key yourself.`,`Have your crew tell the other airmen to stay off it.`,`Tell your commander the other lieutenant ignores the schedule.`,`Talk with the other lieutenant one on one and agree on sharing it.`,`Post a sign up sheet on the equipment for everyone to use.`], a:2, why:`Briefing the boss behind a peer's back is the classic least effective pick. The other lieutenant never got the chance to fix it, and now they learn about the problem from the commander.`, task:`SJ`, domain:`Judgment`}
  ],
  closing:(s,all,G)=>{const wob=(s.flags||{}).wentoverboyd||(G||{}).wentoverboyd;return [
    wob
      ?`At three on Sunday Kit opened her toolbox to put a speed handle back, and the routing slip was lying on the shadow board foam, where she couldn't miss it. The top block was signed. Boyd's door was shut.`
      :`At three on Sunday Boyd came across the shop to her toolbox with the routing slip in his hand. He laid it on the lid, took the pen out of his sleeve pocket, and signed the top block. Then he capped the pen and went back to his office. In eleven years he'd never handed her anything without a word to go with it.`,
    `A. BOYD, MSGT, in blue ink, pressed so hard it had come through on the back. She turned the page over and ran her thumb across the ridges.`,
    `He'd put his name on her toolbox. Under the shadow board foam in there, folded small, was the one piece of paper that could have told him he'd never owed her anything. She'd let him put his name right on top of it.`,
    `On the way out she stopped at the corkboard. Boyd built the drill schedules, and he'd already pinned up the next three weekends, March, April and May, a page each in his block printing. Every launch on every page had a crew chief's name beside it. Every one of them said DARROW.`,
    `Fisk read it over her shoulder. <span class="said" data-who="Fisk">"Guess that puts me on every launch too."</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Kit" data-tone="Dry, plain, uncertain.">"Guess it does."</span>`,
    `She couldn't tell what it was. A man keeping her close for as long as he had her, maybe. Or a man making sure the colonel saw her name on every sortie the wing flew before June. She stood there long enough for Fisk to give up and go get his coat.`,
    `Then she folded the slip in thirds and put it in her chest pocket. The work card from January was still in there, worn at the folds by now, with the five weeks on the back in her own handwriting. She slid the slip in behind it.`
  ]},
  episode:{src:`audio/full-ch02.mp3`, len:`about 35 minutes`},
  next:`Fine Print`
});
